          "text": "## Findings\n\n**Balance is inverted — pads own the mix, the hook does not.**\n\n| Track | Fader | LUFS | Peak | Role in loudness |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pad Wide | 0 dB | **−12.2** | −4.5 | Loudest element |\n| Pad Layer | −1.5 dB | **−13.0** | −5.2 | 2nd loudest |\n| Drums | −1.5 dB | −15.2 | −3.8 | Under the pads |\n| Bass | −3 dB | −15.6 | −7.0 | Under the pads |\n| Arp | −8 dB | −20.2 | −10.0 | Quiet |\n| Lead | **−9.5 dB** | **−22.2** | −12.5 | Quietest — buried |\n\nMaster chorus section sits at −11.2 LUFS / peak −1.2 dB with only ~2.2 dB limiter GR, so this is a *balance* problem, not a loudness-war problem.\n\n**1. Pads mask everything in the mids**\n- Pad Wide spectral: lowMid −14, mid −12, highMid −15 dBFS — dense and wide (width 0.85).\n- Pad Layer: mid −12.5, highMid −14 (width 0.75).\n- Master hottest band is mid_400_2000Hz at −10.5 dB; lowMid is −12.5, nearly tied with the bass band. That mid wall is the two pads with **no EQ** on either chain.\n- Pad Wide also holds a **B2** (pitch 47) for 4 bars of every 8 — low enough to thicken the mud under the bass.\n\n**2. Lead hook is ~10 dB under the pads**\n- Lead only appears bars 25–40 (chorus). Fader −9.5 dB, LUFS −22.2, peak −12.5 (plenty of headroom).\n- Hook lives B4–G5 — same register as Pad Layer (B3/F#4/D5, G3/D4/B4) and the arp (B3–A4). No EQ or presence boost on the lead; nothing is carving space for it.\n\n**3. Chorus on mono bass weakens the low end**\n- Bass is a mono analog-style synth (B1 / G2 / F#2 / E2 8ths) with **Chorus**: rate 0.6 Hz, depth 0.15, mix 0.2.\n- Measured bass width is 0.1 (not fully mono). Chorus on sub/bass fundamentals smears phase and is the clearest “something’s off” candidate in the foundation — especially against drums sub/bass at −14 / −13 dB.\n\n**4. Arp fights the lead in the chorus**\n- Arp runs bars 9–56 at −8 dB with no section automation. Same midrange as the hook, delay send −10 dB — constant competing chatter under the one melodic focal point.\n\n---\n\n## Proposal\n\n1. **Bass › remove Chorus** (or set mix to 0). Restores mono low-end solidity on B1–G2.\n2. **Pad Wide › volume −6 dB** (from 0).\n3. **Pad Layer › volume −7.5 dB** (from −1.5).\n4. **Pad Wide › add EQ**: high-pass 120 Hz (slope 12–24 dB/oct), low-mid cut −3 dB at 250 Hz (Q ≈ 0.8).\n5. **Pad Layer › add EQ**: high-pass 200 Hz, low-mid cut −2 dB at 300 Hz (Q ≈ 0.8).\n6. **Lead › volume −4 dB** (from −9.5) — about +5.5 dB of gain so the hook clears the bed; peak still has room before the master limiter.\n7. **Lead › add EQ**: small presence bell +2.5 dB at 2.5 kHz (Q ≈ 1.2) so it cuts through remaining pad energy.\n8. **Arp › volume automation**: hold −8 dB through verse; drop to **−12 dB** for chorus bars 25–40 (and optionally outro), so the hook has the midrange during its section.\n9. **Pad Wide & Pad Layer › reverb sends**: ease from −6 / −7 dB to **−9 / −10 dB** so the wash doesn’t keep burying the lead after the level cuts.\n\nAfter that, drums/bass should read as the floor, pads as the bed, and the brass lead as the chorus focal point — without needing master-bus heroics.",
